th compression. Union by rank.\n\nEdge list: for each dist pair, compute mutual = max(core[node1], core[node2], dist). Ensure node1 < node2 alphabetically? They are strings p0, p1,... so sort works. Then edges.append((mutual, node1, node2)). Then edges.sort(key=lambda x: (x[0], x[1], x[2])).\n\nKruskal: result_edges = []; for w,u,v in edges: if find(u) != find(v): union(u,v); result_edges.append((u,v,w)); if len(result_edges)==n-1: break. Then check if len(result_edges)==n-1 else raise or return error. But we'll just compute total = sum(w for ...). Return dict.\n\nWe must not mutate case. So we use copy of data? Not needed as we only read.\n\nNow produce JSON string with escaped newlines and quotes.\n\nThe JSON object must be exactly: {\"implementation\": \"...\"}. The Python source code string must be escaped properly.\n\nWe'll write the code in a single line? Better to use multiline string with \\n escapes. We'll write a triple-quoted string but that would complicate JSON escaping. We'll produce a single line with \\n as two characters (backslash n). In JSON string, newline is represented as \\n. So we can write code with actual newlines and then escape them when converting to JSON string. But we need to output JSON directly, so we must escape all special characters. We can construct the string manually.\n\nSimpler: Write the Python code as a multiline string in the answer, then escape it. But I'm generating response text. I'll produce the JSON with the implementation key containing the escaped source.\n\nI'll write the Python code first:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case['nodes']\n    core = case['core_distance']\n    dists = case['distances']\n    n = len(nodes)\n    edges = []\n    for u,v,d in dists:\n        if u > v:\n            u,v = v,u\n        mutual = max(core[u], core[v], d)\n        edges.append((mutual, u, v))\n    edges.sort(key=lambda x: (x[0], x[1], x[2]))\n    parent = {node: node for node in nodes}\n    rank = {node: 0 for node in nodes}\n    def find(x):\n        while parent[x] != x:\n            parent[x] = parent[parent[x]]\n            x = parent[x]\n        return x\n    def union(x,y):\n        rx, ry = find(x), find(y)\n        if rx == ry:\n            return False\n        if rank[rx] < rank[ry]:\n            parent[rx] = ry\n        elif rank[rx] > rank[ry]:\n            parent[ry] = rx\n        else:\n            parent[ry] = rx\n            rank[rx] += 1\n        return True\n    mst_edges = []\n    total = 0.0\n    for w,u,v in edges:\n        if union(u,v):\n            mst_edges.append({'source': u, 'target': v, 'mutual_reachability': w})\n            total += w\n            if len(mst_edges) == n-1:\n                break\n    # Optionally check connectivity, but assume valid\n    return {'edges': mst_edges, 'total_weight': total}\n\nWe need to ensure floating point total is accurate; sum of floats fine.\n\nNow escape for JSON: We'll replace backslashes with double backslash, newlines with \\n, quotes with \\\". But we need to produce the entire JSON object as a string. I'll do it programmatically in my mind.\n\nI'll write the code with actual newlines, then in the response I'll put the JSON with escaped sequences. But since the response is text, I can manually escape. Let me produce the JSON string.\n\nThe code as a single line (with \\n) would be: \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    core = case['core_distance']\\n    dists = case['distances']\\n    n = len(nodes)\\n    edges = []\\n    for u,v,d in dists:\\n        if u > v:\\n            u,v = v,u\\n        mutual = max(core[u], core[v], d)\\n        edges.append((mutual, u, v))\\n    edges.sort(key=lambda x: (x[0], x[1], x[2]))\\n    parent = {node: node for node in nodes}\\n    rank = {node: 0 for node in nodes}\\n    def find(x):\\n        while parent[x] != x:\\n            parent[x] = parent[parent[x]]\\n            x = parent[x]\\n        return x\\n    def union(x,y):\\n        rx, ry = find(x), find(y)\\n  